{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_rqEHBCsu2Cv16MgVnZLtV3f8","content":"Title: kuranov/intervals-icu-tools\nSite: Github\nDate: 2024-01-01\nURL: https://github.com/kuranov/intervals-icu-tools\nSnippet: OAuth2 Authentication (Public Apps). Required for applications serving multiple users. The library handles token management and refresh. import { ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Unable to request narrower scopes in OAuth2 Refresh Token Exchange\nSite: Github\nDate: 2022-08-25\nURL: https://github.com/ory/fosite/issues/696\nSnippet: Reproducing the bug The current unit tests reproduce this issue. For example:\n\n---\n\nTitle: FASTAPI OAuth2 Scopes\nSite: 博客园\nDate: 2022-11-10\nURL: https://www.cnblogs.com/lightsong/p/16877492.html\nSnippet: DEMO 对于用户登录，在可选的scope中，用户选中的scope修饰的API， 用户才有访问权限。\n\n---\n\nTitle: Refresh Tokens & Scopes — OAuth2 & OpenID Connect Deep Dive\nSite: CoddyKit\nDate: 2026-10-05\nURL: https://www.coddykit.com/courses/oauth2_openid/refresh-tokens-scopes-1537906\nSnippet: Free lesson 1 of 3 in OAuth2 & OpenID Connect Deep Dive — after that, PRO unlocks every lesson. Keep Your Apps Authorized Imagine using an app that needs to access your online photos. It gets permission, but what if that permission expires after just an hour? You'd have to log in again and grant permission every single time! That's not a great user experience, is it? The Short Life of Access Tokens Access tokens are like temporary keys. They grant access to specific resources (like your photos) \n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Announcements\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. it doesn't use refresh tokens, only access tokens.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Page 3\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=3\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. These are the scopes: ACTIVITY, WELLNESS, CALENDAR, LIBRARY, SETTINGS\n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth2 Scopes | Quran Foundation Documentation Portal\nSite: quran.foundation\nURL: https://api-docs.quran.foundation/docs/user_related_apis_versioned/scopes/\nSnippet: Standard OAuth2 |Scope|Description| |-|-| |openid|OpenID Connect authentication| |offline_access|Allows obtaining refresh tokens| Content |Scope|Description| |-|-| |content|Read Quran Foundation's non user-related content e.g. Tafsirs, Translations, etc| Search |Scope|Description| |-|-| |search|Search Quran Foundation's indexed content| Analytics |Scope|Description| |-|-| |analytics.events.write|Submit product analytics events using the `client_credentials` grant type; events may optionally incl\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu API Integration Cookbook - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2024-11-20\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-api-integration-cookbook/80090\nSnippet: This tells you everything you need to know to get completed activities into and out of Intervals.icu, to download planned workouts and to read ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Connect Intervals.icu — VéloPeak Wiki\nSite: VéloPeak\nURL: https://velo-peak.com/en/wiki/connect-intervals-icu\nSnippet: VéloPeak connects to Intervals.icu using OAuth 2.0, the same secure standard used by thousands of apps. We never see your Intervals.icu password — you authorise ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth2 Scopes - WSO2 Identity Server Documentation\nSite: wso2.com\nURL: https://is.docs.wso2.com/en/5.9.0/develop/using-oauth2-scopes-rest-apis/\nSnippet: Password Policies - Password History Validation - Password Patterns - Login Policies Login Policies - User Account Locking and Account Disabling User Account Locking and Account Disabling - Overview - Account Locking by Failed Login Attempts - Locking a Specific User Account - Account Disabling - Setting Up ReCaptcha - Workflows Workflows - Workflows - Adding a workflow Engine Adding a workflow Engine - Adding a workflow Engine - Configuring the BPM Profile as a Workflow Engine - Adding a New Wo\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Page 2 - Announcements\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=2\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. There can be many access tokens for an athlete linked to an app, The most recent ...\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_i0a3ZyxyDC6oeMGThm0BARDb","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_y4GobVQKnP56A9X5qePgnHiv","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=3\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_vUcDBV7iLz3tJabTwAJ1qaK9","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://github.com/kuranov/intervals-icu-tools\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_y4GobVQKnP56A9X5qePgnHiv","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## post by Sensitron on Jun 27, 2025\n\nIs a privacy policy mandatory when creating/requesting a new app? I just started developing and dont have one yet. Ofc i could just generate a dummy privacy policy i that is sufficent.\n\n## post by david on Jun 27, 2025\n\nNo you don’t have to start with one. Your app will start only visible to yourself. When you want all users to be able to see it then you need to have a privacy policy in place.\n\n13 days later\n\n\n## post by Sensitron on Jul 11, 2025\n\nHi David,\n\nSorry for being impatient. I sent you an email 11 days ago about the app integration, did you already see it?\n\n## post by david on Jul 11, 2025\n\nNo I didn’t see anything. Please resend to david@intervals.icu and I will sort it out right away.\n\n## post by Sensitron on Jul 11, 2025\n\ndone.\n\n1 month later\n\n\n## post by Mr\\_Jan\\_Exner on Aug 25, 2025\n\nHi David!\n\nCan I ask a question re Scopes? If I want to read athlete data (get /api/v1/athlete/{id}) and gear data (get /api/v1/athlete/{id}/gear{ext}), what scope do I need?\n\nI tried “ATHLETE:READ,GEAR:READ”, but neither exists.\n\nCheers,\n\nJan\n\n## post by david on Aug 25, 2025\n\nThese are the scopes: ACTIVITY, WELLNESS, CALENDAR, LIBRARY, SETTINGS\n\nYou need SETTINGS:READ for those.\n\n## post by Mr\\_Jan\\_Exner on Aug 25, 2025\n\nThanks, that helps!\n\nI can read the athlete, but I get a 405 if I try reading gear.\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/6d9196745d3d46ef00b197e6369dea48.png)\n\nWait… that seems to be my app, because on the documentation site it works, with the same access-token.\n\nI’ll try providing the athlete\\_id…\n\n## post by david on Aug 25, 2025\n\nThere isn’t an endpoint to get an individual bit of gear. You need to use the one that lists all gear for the athlete:\n\n```\nGET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/gear{ext}\n\n```\n\nYou can pass 0 for the athlete id.\n\n## post by Mr\\_Jan\\_Exner on Aug 25, 2025\n\nAh!\n\nAnd the documentation says that, too, urgh.\n\nThanks for your patience, David!\n\n1 month later\n\n\n## post by david on Sep 30, 2025\n\nOAuth apps can now subscribe to webhooks for chat messages and so on. Note that groups are also chats so this support includes group messages.\n\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/e4806956da2eb76d7570d0d3ae41f650.png)\n\nSample new message payload:\n\n```lang-auto\n{\n  \"secret\": \"...\",\n  \"events\": [\n    {\n      \"athlete_id\": \"2049151\",\n      \"type\": \"CHAT_UPDATE\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2025-09-30T07:18:59.147+00:00\",\n      \"payload\": {\n        \"type\": \"MESSAGES\",\n        \"chat_id\": 2741,\n        \"new_message_count\": 1,\n        \"messages\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": 2961313,\n            \"athlete_id\": \"xxx\",\n            \"name\": \"xxx\",\n            \"created\": \"2025-09-30T07:18:59.063+00:00\",\n            \"type\": \"TEXT\",\n            \"content\": \"bar\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n```\n\nSample new chat payload (another athlete has initiated a chat):\n\n```lang-auto\n{\n  \"secret\": \"...\",\n  \"events\": [\n    {\n      \"athlete_id\": \"2049151\",\n      \"type\": \"CHAT_UPDATE\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2025-09-30T07:22:59.005+00:00\",\n      \"payload\": {\n        \"type\": \"CHATS\",\n        \"chats\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": 1036589,\n            \"type\": \"PRIVATE\",\n            \"updated\": \"2025-09-30T07:22:58.986+00:00\",\n            \"name\": \"John xxx\",\n            \"picture\": \"https://...\",\n            \"pub\": false,\n            \"hide_members\": false,\n            \"members_cannot_chat\": false,\n            \"other_athlete_id\": \"xxx\",\n            \"other_athlete_sex\": \"M\",\n            \"role\": \"ADMIN\",\n            \"new_message_count\": 1,\n            \"last_seen_message_id\": 0\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n```\n\nCombine this with the chat endpoints to build Intervals.icu chat into your applications.\n\n## post by Mark\\_Boulton on Oct 1, 2025\n\nHi David - i’m building an OAuth integration for iOS and I continue to hit this error when trying to authenticate. I can confirm my details are correct when trying so, and i’m pretty stumped as to what is causing this. What is the ‘network error’ error raised for?\n\n\n![image3](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/f86955bd7eb5ff29b211542731e1a144.jpeg)\n\n## post by david on Oct 1, 2025\n\nI don’t know what that is. Can you login from a normal Safari window?\n\n## post by Mark\\_Boulton on Oct 1, 2025\n\nYes, I can and it works fine. I was wondering if this is a known issue for some reason on iOS.\n\n## post by david on Oct 1, 2025\n\nNot that I am aware of. There are several IOS apps that connect with Intervals.icu. Maybe some settings in the web view are blocking network calls? The login is just a JSON POST to an Intervals.icu endpoint.\n\n## post by Mark\\_Boulton on Oct 1, 2025\n\nOk thanks. I’ll investigate further and if i fix it i’ll post it back - just in case anyone else experiences it.\n\n## post by Mark\\_Boulton on Oct 1, 2025\n\n4 months later\n\n\n## Split this topic on 23 Jan\n\n## post by Ruud\\_Goorden on 26 Jan\n\n## post by david on 26 Jan"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_vUcDBV7iLz3tJabTwAJ1qaK9","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## @kuranov/intervals-client\n\nPermalink: @kuranov/intervals-client\n\nModern, type-safe TypeScript client for the Intervals.icu API.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\nPermalink: Table of Contents\n\n- Why This Library?\n- Install\n- Quick Start\n  - API Key Authentication\n  - OAuth2 Authentication\n- API Coverage\n  - Resources\n  - Quick Reference\n- Configuration\n  - Retry with jitter\n  - Hooks for observability\n- Examples\n  - Working with activities\n  - Working with events\n  - Working with athletes\n  - Working with wellness\n  - OAuth2 Flow\n  - Working with workout library\n- Error Handling\n  - Result Pattern\n  - Error Types\n- Type Safety & Validation\n  - Validation Philosophy\n- Contributing\n- Development\n- License\n- Acknowledgments\n\n## Why This Library?\n\nPermalink: Why This Library?\n\n- **🛡️ Runtime Validation** \\- Catches API changes before they break your app (powered by Valibot)\n- **🌍 Universal** \\- Works in Node.js 18+, Bun, Deno, browsers, and edge runtimes\n- **🪶 Lightweight** \\- Zero heavy dependencies (~12KB gzipped)\n- **🔐 Auth Ready** \\- Supports both API key and OAuth2 authentication\n- **⚡ Smart Rate Limiting** \\- Automatic retry with exponential backoff\n- **📦 Extensive Coverage** \\- 64/146 API endpoints (44%): Activities, Events, Athletes, Wellness, Library, Chats\n- **🎯 Type Safe** \\- Full TypeScript support with strict types\n- **🧪 Well Tested** \\- 125 tests covering all operations\n\n## Install\n\nPermalink: Install\n\n```\nnpm install @kuranov/intervals-client\n# or\npnpm add @kuranov/intervals-client\n# or\nyarn add @kuranov/intervals-client\n```\n\n## Quick Start\n\nPermalink: Quick Start\n\n### API Key Authentication (Personal Scripts)\n\nPermalink: API Key Authentication (Personal Scripts)\n\nPerfect for personal automation scripts and server-side integrations.\n\n```\nimport { IntervalsClient } from '@kuranov/intervals-client';\n\nconst client = new IntervalsClient({\n  auth: { type: 'apiKey', apiKey: process.env.INTERVALS_API_KEY! },\n});\n\n// List recent activities\nconst result = await client.activities.list(\"i12345\", {\n  oldest: '2024-01-01',\n  limit: 10,\n});\n\nif (!result.ok) {\n  console.error('Error:', result.error.kind);\n  process.exitCode = 1;\n} else {\n  console.log(`Found ${result.value.length} activities`);\n  result.value.forEach(activity => {\n    console.log(`${activity.startDateLocal}: ${activity.name}`);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### OAuth2 Authentication (Public Apps)\n\nPermalink: OAuth2 Authentication (Public Apps)\n\nRequired for applications serving multiple users. The library handles token management and refresh.\n\n```\nimport { IntervalsClient } from '@kuranov/intervals-client';\n\nconst client = new IntervalsClient({\n  auth: {\n    type: 'accessToken',\n    accessToken: user.intervalsAccessToken,\n  },\n});\n\n// Get athlete profile\nconst athlete = await client.athletes.get('me');\nif (athlete.ok) {\n  console.log(`Athlete: ${athlete.value.name}`);\n  console.log(`FTP: ${athlete.value.ftp || 'Not set'}`);\n}\n```\n\n> **Note:** OAuth2 flow requires separate handling of authorization URL generation and token exchange. See the OAuth Guide below for complete implementation.\n\n## API Coverage\n\nPermalink: API Coverage\n\nv1.1.0 provides **44% coverage** (64/146 endpoints) of the Intervals.icu API with complete support for core resources:\n\n### Resources\n\nPermalink: Resources\n\n- **Activities** (39/39 endpoints, 100%) - List, get, update, delete activities, intervals, streams, power curves, search\n- **Events** (17/17 endpoints, 100%) - Manage calendar events, workouts, plans, downloads\n- **Chats** (8/8 endpoints, 100%) - Messages, activity comments, notifications\n- **Athletes** (~10 endpoints) - Get athlete profiles, settings, and summaries\n- **Wellness** (~6 endpoints) - Track daily wellness metrics (HRV, sleep, mood, etc.)\n- **Library** (~10 endpoints) - Manage workout library folders and plans\n\n### Quick Reference\n\nPermalink: Quick Reference\n\n```\n// Activities - Basic operations\nawait client.activities.list(athleteId, { oldest: '2024-01-01' });\nawait client.activities.get(activityId);\nawait client.activities.update(activityId, { name: 'Morning Ride' });\nawait client.activities.delete(activityId);\n\n// Activities - Streams & analysis (NEW in v1.1.0)\nawait client.activities.getStreams(activityId, { types: ['watts', 'heartrate'] });\nawait client.activities.getPowerCurve(activityId);\nawait client.activities.search(athleteId, 'race');\nawait client.activities.downloadFitFile(activityId);\n\n// Events (Calendar)\nawait client.events.list(athleteId, { oldest: '2024-01-01' });\nawait client.events.create(athleteId, { name: 'Workout', startDateLocal: '2024-01-15' });\nawait client.events.update(athleteId, eventId, { description: 'Updated' });\nawait client.events.downloadWorkout(athleteId, eventId, 'fit'); // NEW\nawait client.events.applyPlan(athleteId, { folderId: 123, oldest: '2024-01-01', newest: '2024-12-31' }); // NEW\n\n// Chats - Messages & comments (NEW in v1.1.0)\nawait client.chats.list(athleteId);\nawait client.chats.sendMessage({ chatId: 123, text: 'Great workout!' });\nawait client.chats.listActivityMessages(activityId);\nawait client.chats.addActivityMessage(activityId, 'Nice effort!');\n\n// Wellness\nawait client.wellness.list(athleteId, { oldest: '2024-01-01' });\nawait client.wellness.update(athleteId, '2024-01-15', { weight: 72, restingHR: 45 });\n\n// Athletes\nawait client.athletes.get(athleteId);\nawait client.athletes.update(athleteId, { name: 'New Name' });\nawait client.athletes.getSettings(athleteId);\n\n// Library\nawait client.library.listWorkouts(athleteId);\nawait client.library.listFolders(athleteId);\nawait client.library.createFolder(athleteId, { type: 'FOLDER', name: 'My Workouts' });\n```\n\nSee Examples below for complete usage patterns.\n\n## Configuration\n\nPermalink: Configuration\n\n### Retry with jitter\n\nPermalink: Retry with jitter\n\nAutomatically retries rate-limited requests (429) with exponential backoff and optional jitter:\n\n```\nconst client = new IntervalsClient({\n  auth: { type: 'apiKey', apiKey: process.env.INTERVALS_API_KEY! },\n  retry: {\n    limit: 3,              // Max retries (default: 3)\n    initialDelayMs: 1000,  // Base delay (default: 1000ms)\n    maxDelayMs: 8000,      // Max delay cap (default: 8000ms)\n    jitter: true,          // Add randomness (default: true)\n    jitterFactor: 0.2,     // ±20% variation (default: 0.2)\n  },\n});\n```\n\nJitter prevents \"thundering herd\" when many clients retry simultaneously. The library automatically respects `Retry-After` headers when present.\n\n### Hooks for observability\n\nPermalink: Hooks for observability\n\nAdd logging, metrics, or monitoring with lifecycle hooks:\n\n```\nconst client = new IntervalsClient({\n  auth: { type: 'apiKey', apiKey: process.env.INTERVALS_API_KEY! },\n  hooks: {\n    onRequest: ({ method, path }) => {\n      console.log(`→ ${method} ${path}`);\n    },\n    onResponse: ({ method, path, status, durationMs }) => {\n      console.log(`← ${method} ${path} ${status} (${durationMs}ms)`);\n    },\n    onError: ({ method, path, error, durationMs }) => {\n      console.error(`✗ ${method} ${path} failed after ${durationMs}ms:`, error);\n    },\n    onRetry: ({ method, path, attempt, maxAttempts, delayMs, reason }) => {\n      console.log(`⟳ ${method} ${path} retry ${attempt}/${maxAttempts} after ${delayMs}ms (${reason})`);\n    },\n  },\n});\n```\n\nAll hooks support both sync and async functions. **Note:** If a hook throws an error, it will fail the request (except `onError`, which is swallowed to avoid masking the original error).\n\n## Examples\n\nPermalink: Examples\n\n### Working with activities\n\nPermalink: Working with activities\n\n```\n// Update an activity\nconst updated = await client.activities.update(123456, {\n  name: 'Morning Run',\n  description: 'Easy recovery run',\n});\n\n// Delete an activity\nconst deleted = await client.activities.delete(123456);\n\n// Get intervals for an activity\nconst intervals = await client.activities.getIntervals(123456);\n\n// Update intervals\nconst updatedIntervals = await client.activities.updateIntervals(123456, [\n  { type: 'ACTIVE', start: 0, end: 300, averageWatts: 250 },\n  { type: 'REST', start: 300, end: 420, averageWatts: 100 },\n]);\n\n// Split an interval at a specific point\nconst split = await client.activities.splitInterval(123456, 150);\n```\n\n### Working with events\n\nPermalink: Working with events\n\n```\n// List events (planned workouts, notes, etc.)\nconst events = await client.events.list(\"i12345\", {\n  oldest: '2024-01-01',\n  newest: '2024-01-31',\n  category: ['WORKOUT', 'NOTES'],\n});\n\n// Get a single event\nconst event = await client.events.get(\"i12345\", 789);\n\n// Create an event\nconst newEvent = await client.events.create(\"i12345\", {\n  name: 'Morning Workout',\n  category: 'WORKOUT',\n  start_date_local: '2024-01-15',\n  description: '5x5min @ FTP',\n});\n\n// Update an event\nconst updated = await client.events.update(\"i12345\", 789, {\n  name: 'Updated Workout Name',\n});\n\n// Delete an event\nconst deleted = await client.events.delete(\"i12345\", 789);\n\n// Bulk create events\nconst bulkEvents = await client.events.createMultiple(\"i12345\", [\n  { name: 'Workout 1', start_date_local: '2024-01-15', category: 'WORKOUT' },\n  { name: 'Workout 2', start_date_local: '2024-01-16', category: 'WORKOUT' },\n]);\n\n// Bulk delete events\nconst bulkDeleted = await client.events.deleteBulk(\"i12345\", [\n  { id: 100 },\n  { id: 101 },\n  { external_id: 'external-123' },\n]);\n\n// Update multiple events in a date range\nconst updatedRange = await client.events.updateMultiple(\n  \"i12345\",\n  { hide_from_athlete: true },\n  { oldest: '2024-01-01', newest: '2024-01-31' }\n);\n\n// List event tags\nconst tags = await client.events.listTags(\"i12345\");\n```\n\n### Working with athletes\n\nPermalink: Working with athletes\n\n```\n// Get athlete with sport settings\nconst athlete = await client.athletes.get(\"i12345\");\nif (athlete.ok) {\n  console.log(athlete.value.name);\n  console.log(athlete.value.sportSettings);\n}\n\n// Update athlete\nconst updated = await client.athletes.update(\"i12345\", {\n  name: 'Updated Name',\n  weight: 72,\n  timezone: 'America/New_York',\n});\n\n// Get athlete settings for a device\nconst desktopSettings = await client.athletes.getSettings(\"i12345\", 'desktop');\nconst phoneSettings = await client.athletes.getSettings(\"i12345\", 'phone');\nconst tabletSettings = await client.athletes.getSettings(\"i12345\", 'tablet');\n\n// Get athlete profile (includes FTP, thresholds, etc.)\nconst profile = await client.athletes.getProfile(\"i12345\");\nif (profile.ok) {\n  console.log(`FTP: ${profile.value.ftp}`);\n  console.log(`Max HR: ${profile.value.maxHeartrate}`);\n  console.log(`Threshold Power: ${profile.value.thresholdPower}`);\n}\n\n// Get athlete summary (CTL, ATL, TSB, etc.)\nconst summary = await client.athletes.getSummary(\"i12345\", {\n  start: '2024-01-01',\n  end: '2024-01-31',\n  tags: ['cycling', 'running'],\n});\nif (summary.ok) {\n  summary.value.forEach((s) => {\n    console.log(`${s.startDateLocal}: CTL=${s.ctl}, ATL=${s.atl}, TSB=${s.tsb}`);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### Working with wellness\n\nPermalink: Working with wellness\n\n```\n// List wellness records for a date range\nconst records = await client.wellness.list(\"i12345\", {\n  oldest: '2024-01-01',\n  newest: '2024-01-31',\n});\nif (records.ok) {\n  records.value.forEach((w) => {\n    console.log(`${w.id}: weight=${w.weight}kg, HR=${w.restingHR}`);\n  });\n}\n\n// Get wellness for a specific date\nconst wellness = await client.wellness.get(\"i12345\", '2024-01-15');\nif (wellness.ok) {\n  console.log(`Weight: ${wellness.value.weight}kg`);\n  console.log(`Resting HR: ${wellness.value.restingHR}bpm`);\n  console.log(`HRV: ${wellness.value.hrv}`);\n  console.log(`Sleep: ${wellness.value.sleepSecs}s`);\n}\n\n// Update wellness for a date\nconst updated = await client.wellness.update(\"i12345\", '2024-01-15', {\n  weight: 71.5,\n  sleepSecs: 28800, // 8 hours\n  soreness: 3,\n  fatigue: 2,\n  mood: 8,\n  comments: 'Feeling good today',\n});\n\n// Bulk update multiple wellness records\nconst bulkUpdate = await client.wellness.updateBulk(\"i12345\", [\n  { id: '2024-01-15', weight: 70.5, restingHR: 55 },\n  { id: '2024-01-16', weight: 70.3, restingHR: 54 },\n  { id: '2024-01-17', weight: 70.1, restingHR: 53 },\n]);\n```\n\n### OAuth2 Flow\n\nPermalink: OAuth2 Flow\n\nFor public applications serving multiple users, implement the full OAuth2 authorization code flow:\n\n```\n// Step 1: Redirect user to authorization URL\nconst authUrl = new URL('https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize');\nauthUrl.searchParams.set('client_id', YOUR_CLIENT_ID);\nauthUrl.searchParams.set('redirect_uri', YOUR_REDIRECT_URI);\nauthUrl.searchParams.set('response_type', 'code');\nauthUrl.searchParams.set('scope', 'ACTIVITY:READ ACTIVITY:WRITE WELLNESS:READ');\nauthUrl.searchParams.set('state', generateRandomState()); // CSRF protection\n\n// Redirect user to: authUrl.toString()\n\n// Step 2: Handle callback and exchange code for token\nconst tokenResponse = await fetch('https://intervals.icu/api/v1/oauth/token', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({\n    grant_type: 'authorization_code',\n    code: codeFromCallback,\n    client_id: YOUR_CLIENT_ID,\n    client_secret: YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET,\n    redirect_uri: YOUR_REDIRECT_URI,\n  }),\n});\n\nconst tokens = await tokenResponse.json();\n// { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, token_type, scope }\n\n// Step 3: Use the access token\nconst client = new IntervalsClient({\n  auth: { type: 'accessToken', accessToken: tokens.access_token },\n});\n\n// Step 4: Refresh when expired (tokens expire after 8 hours)\nconst refreshResponse = await fetch('https://intervals.icu/api/v1/oauth/token', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({\n    grant_type: 'refresh_token',\n    refresh_token: tokens.refresh_token,\n    client_id: YOUR_CLIENT_ID,\n    client_secret: YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET,\n  }),\n});\n\nconst newTokens = await refreshResponse.json();\n// Update stored tokens and recreate client with new access_token\n```\n\n**Available OAuth scopes:**\n\n- `ACTIVITY:READ`, `ACTIVITY:WRITE`\n- `WELLNESS:READ`, `WELLNESS:WRITE`\n- `CALENDAR:READ`, `CALENDAR:WRITE`\n- `SETTINGS:READ`\n\n### Working with workout library\n\nPermalink: Working with workout library\n\n```\n// List all workouts in your library\nconst workouts = await client.library.listWorkouts(\"i12345\");\nif (workouts.ok) {\n  workouts.value.forEach((w) => {\n    console.log(`${w.name} (${w.activityType})`);\n  });\n}\n\n// Get a specific workout\nconst workout = await client.library.getWorkout(\"i12345\", 123);\nif (workout.ok) {\n  console.log(`Workout: ${workout.value.name}`);\n  console.log(`Description: ${workout.value.description}`);\n}\n\n// Create a new workout\nconst newWorkout = await client.library.createWorkout(\"i12345\", {\n  name: '5x5min @ FTP',\n  description: '5 x 5min @ FTP with 3min recovery',\n  folder_id: 10,\n  activity_type: 'Ride',\n  tags: ['intervals', 'ftp'],\n});\n\n// Update a workout\nconst updated = await client.library.updateWorkout(\"i12345\", 123, {\n  name: 'Updated Workout Name',\n  description: 'New description',\n});\n\n// Delete a workout\nconst deleted = await client.library.deleteWorkout(\"i12345\", 123);\n\n// Create multiple workouts at once\nconst bulkWorkouts = await client.library.createMultipleWorkouts(\"i12345\", [\n  { name: 'Workout 1', folder_id: 10, activity_type: 'Ride' },\n  { name: 'Workout 2', folder_id: 10, activity_type: 'Ride' },\n]);\n\n// List folders and plans\nconst folders = await client.library.listFolders(\"i12345\");\nif (folders.ok) {\n  folders.value.forEach((f) => {\n    console.log(`${f.name} (${f.type})`);\n    console.log(`  Workouts: ${f.children?.length || 0}`);\n  });\n}\n\n// Create a folder\nconst newFolder = await client.library.createFolder(\"i12345\", {\n  type: 'FOLDER',\n  name: 'Cycling Workouts',\n  description: 'My cycling library',\n  visibility: 'PRIVATE',\n});\n\n// Create a training plan\nconst newPlan = await client.library.createFolder(\"i12345\", {\n  type: 'PLAN',\n  name: 'Base Building Plan',\n  description: '12-week aerobic base',\n  visibility: 'PUBLIC',\n  rollout_weeks: 12,\n  auto_rollout_day: 1, // Monday\n  starting_ctl: 50,\n  starting_atl: 40,\n});\n\n// Update a folder\nconst updatedFolder = await client.library.updateFolder(\"i12345\", 10, {\n  name: 'Updated Folder Name',\n  description: 'New description',\n});\n\n// Delete a folder\nconst deletedFolder = await client.library.deleteFolder(\"i12345\", 10);\n\n// List all workout tags\nconst tags = await client.library.listTags(\"i12345\");\nif (tags.ok) {\n  console.log('Tags:', tags.value.join(', '));\n}\n```\n\n## Error Handling\n\nPermalink: Error Handling\n\nAll API calls return a `Result<T, ApiError>` \\- no exceptions are thrown.\n\n### Result Pattern\n\nPermalink: Result Pattern\n\n```\nconst result = await client.activities.get(123);\n\nif (!result.ok) {\n  // Error handling\n  switch (result.error.kind) {\n    case 'Unauthorized':\n      console.error('Invalid credentials');\n      break;\n    case 'NotFound':\n      console.error('Activity not found');\n      break;\n    case 'Schema':\n      console.error('Validation failed:', result.error.issues);\n      break;\n    case 'RateLimit':\n      console.error('Rate limited, retry after:', result.error.retryAfter);\n      break;\n    default:\n      console.error('Unknown error:', result.error);\n  }\n} else {\n  // Success - use result.value\n  console.log(result.value.name);\n}\n```\n\n### Error Types\n\nPermalink: Error Types\n\n- `Unauthorized` (401) - Invalid API key or expired token\n- `Forbidden` (403) - Insufficient permissions\n- `NotFound` (404) - Resource doesn't exist\n- `RateLimit` (429) - Too many requests (includes `retryAfter` hint)\n- `Schema` \\- Response validation failed (API changed or malformed data)\n- `Network` \\- Connection error, timeout, or DNS failure\n- `Unknown` \\- Other HTTP errors\n\n## Type Safety & Validation\n\nPermalink: Type Safety & Validation\n\nThis library uses Valibot for runtime schema validation and TypeScript type generation.\n\n### Validation Philosophy\n\nPermalink: Validation Philosophy\n\nThe library uses a **\"loose object\"** validation strategy that balances type safety with API flexibility:\n\n```\n// Schema example (simplified)\nexport const ActivitySchema = v.looseObject({\n  // Required fields - API always returns these\n  id: v.union([v.string(), v.number()]),\n  type: v.string(),\n  startDateLocal: v.string(),\n\n  // Optional fields - may or may not be present\n  name: v.optional(v.string()),\n  distance: v.optional(v.number()),\n  // ... 100+ more optional fields\n});\n```\n\n#### Why `looseObject`?\n\nPermalink: Why looseObject?\n\n**Reason 1: API Evolution**\nThe Intervals.icu API can return extra fields not documented in the OpenAPI spec. Using `looseObject` allows the library to accept these fields without breaking.\n\n**Reason 2: Incomplete Documentation**\nSome API endpoints return fields that aren't in the official spec. Strict validation would reject valid API responses.\n\n**Reason 3: Future Compatibility**\nWhen Intervals.icu adds new fields, your code continues working without requiring a library update.\n\n#### What Gets Validated?\n\nPermalink: What Gets Validated?\n\n✅ **Required fields are enforced:**\n\n```\n// TypeScript knows these are always present\nactivity.id         // number | string (never undefined)\nactivity.type       // string (never undefined)\nactivity.startDateLocal  // string (never undefined)\n```\n\n✅ **Optional fields are typed correctly:**\n\n```\n// TypeScript knows these might be undefined\nactivity.name       // string | undefined\nactivity.distance   // number | undefined\n```\n\n✅ **Invalid data is caught at runtime:**\n\n```\nconst result = await client.activities.get(123);\n\nif (!result.ok && result.error.kind === 'Schema') {\n  // API returned data that doesn't match the schema\n  console.error('Validation failed:', result.error.issues);\n}\n```\n\n❌ **Extra fields are allowed (not validated):**\n\n```\n// If API returns a new field \"future_field\", it passes validation\n// and is available on the object (but TypeScript doesn't know about it)\n```\n\n#### Trade-offs\n\nPermalink: Trade-offs\n\n**Benefits:**\n\n- ✅ Forward compatible with API changes\n- ✅ Works with undocumented API fields\n- ✅ No false-positive validation errors\n- ✅ Simple, consistent pattern across all schemas\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n- ⚠️ Extra fields aren't typed in TypeScript (use type assertions if needed)\n- ⚠️ Can't catch typos in field names at runtime (TypeScript catches them at compile time)\n\n#### When Validation Fails\n\nPermalink: When Validation Fails\n\nIf the API returns data that doesn't match the schema (e.g., missing required field, wrong type), you'll get a `Schema` error:\n\n```\nconst result = await client.activities.get(123);\n\nif (!result.ok) {\n  if (result.error.kind === 'Schema') {\n    // Valibot validation failed\n    console.error('Schema validation error:');\n    result.error.issues.forEach(issue => {\n      console.error(`  ${issue.path}: ${issue.message}`);\n    });\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is rare but can happen if:\n\n- The Intervals.icu API changes in a breaking way\n- There's a bug in the API\n- Network corruption (very rare)\n\n## Contributing\n\nPermalink: Contributing\n\nContributions are welcome! Please:\n\n1. Open an issue to discuss the change\n2. Fork the repository\n3. Create a feature branch\n4. Add tests for new functionality\n5. Ensure all tests pass ( `pnpm test`)\n6. Submit a pull request\n\n## Development\n\nPermalink: Development\n\n```\n# Install dependencies\npnpm install\n\n# Type check\npnpm run typecheck\n\n# Run tests\npnpm test\n\n# Build\npnpm run build\n```\n\n## License\n\nPermalink: License\n\nMIT © Anton Kuranov\n\n## Acknowledgments\n\nPermalink: Acknowledgments\n\nBuilt for the Intervals.icu community. Special thanks to David Tinker for creating and maintaining the platform and its open API."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_i0a3ZyxyDC6oeMGThm0BARDb","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\nIntervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an Intervals.icu user’s data. It allows users to grant and revoke API access on a per-application basis and keeps user authentication details safe.\n\n## Heading link Creating An Application\n\nFill in the application form here: https://intervals.icu/oauth/apply\n\nYou need to be logged in as the Intervals.icu athlete that will own the app. This athlete will have access to the management page to edit the app, setup redirect URI’s, webhooks and other things.\n\nYour app will show up on the /settings/apps page (only for you as the owner) and you can click “Manage App” to retrieve your client\\_id and secret, change redirect URL’s, configure webhooks and so on.\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/cb935eaa9a1b9e784347f5a9486fe24a.png)\n\nThe app will have status “Pending” until it is approved. **Please note that you will not be able to do the OAuth flow until it is approved**. You can upload a logo, get the client ID and secret and so on.\n\nOnce it has been approved, the pending tag will disappear. You will receive a system notification when this happens (delivered by email and in the Intervals.icu app).\n\nNote that it will not be visible to other users in /settings/apps, but you can send anyone you like to the OAuth consent page and begin integration.\n\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/ef7703e10049bf07ae1b507e4796aacc.png)\n\nWhen you have completed your integration and tested properly, contact support@intervals.icu to have it listed in the public app directory. Please include a link to the management page in the email.\n\n## Heading link Requesting Authorization To Access Intervals.icu Data For A User\n\nSend the user to:\n\n```lang-auto\nhttps://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=<your client id>\n    &redirect_uri=<your redirect uri>\n    &scope=<required scopes>\n    &state=<optional data>\n\n```\n\nIntervals.icu will ask the user to login and display a confirmation dialog with options to choose which\n\nscopes to grant the application. If the user confirms then they are redirected to the redirect\\_uri with an\n\nauthorization code and the optional state parameter:\n\n```lang-auto\n<your redirect uri>?code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4&state=...\n\n```\n\nIf they decline:\n\n```lang-auto\n<your redirect uri>?error=access_denied\n\n```\n\nYour server needs to exchange the code for an access token within 2 minutes by POSTing form data including your client\\_id and client\\_secret:\n\n```lang-auto\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=... \\\n    -d client_secret=... \\\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n\n```\n\nIf all goes well Intervals.icu will respond with an access token, granted scopes and the id and name of the athlete:\n\n```lang-auto\n{\n    \"token_type\": \"Bearer\",\n    \"access_token\": \"d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9\",\n    \"scope\": \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:WRITE\",\n    \"athlete\": {\n        \"id\": \"2049151\",\n        \"name\": \"David (intervals.icu)\"\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\nTo call the API use “Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9” header.\n\nNote that you can use “0” for the athlete id for endpoints that accept an athlete id in the path. This will use the athlete for the bearer token used to make the call.\n\n### Heading link PKCE (optional)\n\nPKCE is supported to protect the authorization code from interception. Generate a random `code_verifier` (43-128 chars of `A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~`) and add its challenge to the authorize URL (only `S256` is supported):\n\n```\n    &code_challenge=<BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier)) without padding>\n    &code_challenge_method=S256\n\n```\n\nThen include `-d code_verifier=...` when exchanging the code. If a challenge was sent, the verifier is required, and a verifier is rejected if no challenge was sent. The client\\_secret is still required unless your app is a public client.\n\nOnce every version of your app uses PKCE, tick “Require PKCE” on your app’s settings page. Authorizations without a code\\_challenge are then rejected, and so is exchanging a code without a code\\_verifier, even with the client\\_secret.\n\n### Heading link Public clients (SPA, mobile and desktop apps)\n\nApps that cannot keep a client\\_secret confidential can be flagged as public clients on the OAuth app management page. Public clients that use PKCE can omit client\\_secret when exchanging the code. Without PKCE the client\\_secret is still required (this is supported so existing apps can migrate, please switch to PKCE). The automatic http://localhost redirect exception does not apply to public clients, so register the exact redirect URIs you use, including any localhost ones. The port of a registered http://localhost or http://127.0.0.1 redirect is ignored, so native apps can listen on any free port:\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=...\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n    -d code_verifier=...\n\n```\n\n## Heading link Scopes\n\nScopes are as follows:\n\n- ACTIVITY: Completed rides, runs etc.\n- WELLNESS: Weight, resting HR etc.\n- CALENDAR: Planned workouts\n- CHATS: Chats, groups and messages\n- LIBRARY: Workout library\n- SETTINGS: Athlete settings\n\nFor each scope specify READ or WRITE (to update, implies READ access) and use commas to separate multiple scopes. Example:\n\n```lang-auto\nACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:WRITE\n\n```\n\nRequests read access to activities and read and write access to wellness data.\n\n## Heading link Revoking An Access Token\n\nWhen a user disconnects Intervals.icu in your app or deletes their data on your side, revoke the access token:\n\n```lang-auto\ncurl -X DELETE \"https://intervals.icu/api/v1/disconnect-app\" \\\n -H 'authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThis will stop webhook delivery for the athlete.\n\n## Heading link Your Own Data\n\nNote that you don’t need to do all this if you just want access to your own data. Use your API key to do that.\n\n## Heading link API Endpoints"}
{"role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_7BCGg6r661jA1trCGWCpfmNd","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=2\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_7BCGg6r661jA1trCGWCpfmNd","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## post by pmcarlos on Aug 31, 2022\n\nThanks for your response, I’ll update my app accordingly then\n\n## post by david on Sep 2, 2022\n\nIt is as app4g says. I am planning to support multiple access tokens per app soon. That was an oversight on my part.\n\n1 year later\n\n\n## post by Leonid\\_Kholkine on Sep 21, 2023\n\nComing back to this, is multi access token already supported?\n\nThanks!\n\n10 days later\n\n\n## post by david on Oct 1, 2023\n\nI just implemented that. There can be many access tokens for an athlete linked to an app, once for each time they authorise it. The most recent scopes apply to all access tokens.\n\n1 month later\n\n\n## post by Leonid\\_Kholkine on Nov 7, 2023\n\nThanks! One more question, is there also a scope for managing my athletes? The use case it’s that coaches will be connecting to intervals.\n\nEDIT: Found it in Swagger, there is a ATHLETES scope, but still doesn’t seem to work.\n\nEDIT2: It seems accessing /athletes seems to work with my own API key but not through the bearer token where I get 403.\n\n## post by david on Nov 8, 2023\n\nCurrently oauth tokens grant access to the authorising athlete’s data but not to people they follow or coach. This is by design so someone who follows you can’t auth a 3rd party app which then gains access to your data. I think you need to get each athlete to auth your app and then use the correct token depending on the athlete.\n\nWill this work?\n\n## post by Leonid\\_Kholkine on Nov 8, 2023\n\nThe only (specific) issue to us, it’s that we work with coaches and use there data. Unfortunatly our whole data platform is built around the idea of 1 connection → multiple athletes. This was following the same logic as TrainingPeaks basically, where the coached athletes are also available through the API.\n\n7 months later\n\n\n## post by Aart\\_Goossens on Jun 12, 2024\n\nIs there a way to have intervals.icu push new activities for a user to an application?\n\nAlternatively, I could regularly poll the API for new activities. Is there a rate limit in place that would limit this solution?\n\nThe rate limiting question is also relevant for the backfill scenario (where users first authorize the application and where the applications needs to process historical activities). Are there any limitations on doing that?\n\n## post by david on Jun 13, 2024\n\n\nThere are are rate limits but they are very generous and you aren’t likely to get a 429. They are there mostly to protect the server app from bugs in the Intervals.icu client\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/39a7d733bfd9e00ee3fd31c2b41c7238.png)\n\nSo backfill shouldn’t be a problem.\n\nThere is some undocumented support for webhooks. If you send me one or two web hook URLs (e.g. one dev one prod). I will set that up for you.\n\n## post by Aart\\_Goossens on Jun 13, 2024\n\nThanks for the reply. I’ll reach out to you as soon as I’m ready for integration!\n\n6 months later\n\n\n## post by david on Dec 6, 2024\n\nThere is now a management page for each OAuth application. You can view your client\\_id and secret, change redirect URL’s, enable webhooks and so on. Apps that you own on the /settings page get a “Manage App” button:\n\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/ef7703e10049bf07ae1b507e4796aacc.png)\n\n![image3](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/f5938d5f8d27d450a374fc2a82cec171.png)\n\n2 months later\n\n\n## post by John\\_Peters\\_endura.f on Feb 4, 2025\n\nHi David - just noticed this. Does this mean you have webhooks in intervals now as well as the REST API?\n\nCheers\n\nJohn\n\n## post by david on Feb 4, 2025\n\nYes that is correct. You can just tick the boxes for the ones you want to receive.\n\n## post by John\\_Peters\\_endura.f on Feb 4, 2025\n\nJust trying to test this using the wellness updated endpoint. Is it a POST method required at my end to receive the data? I don’t seem to be getting anything coming through at the moment when I change my wellness data\n\n## post by david on Feb 4, 2025\n\nYes it is a POST. I forgot to mention that in the doc and have just added it.\n\n## post by John\\_Peters\\_endura.f on Feb 4, 2025\n\nThanks. Is there an easy way of figuring out what I’m doing wrong. My endpoints work testing in postman when I send a json file (I’m just dumping it in a DB for the moment), but I’m not getting anything through from Intervals when I change my calendar or wellness data.\n\n## post by david on Feb 4, 2025\n\nIts giving a 404:\n\n> 2025-02-04 14:12:29.248 DEBUG 1 — \\[ok-publisher-12\\] i.i.service.WebhookPublisherService : Error posting webhook i68403:WELLNESS\\_UPDATED:2025-02-04 xxx/, retry(4): icu.intervals.util.HttpStatusCodeException: Got 404 {“error”:“404 - Resource Not Found”}\n\nThat xxx is your webhook URL. Realised I shouldn’t post that here!\n\n3 months later\n\n\n## post by Aart\\_Goossens on Apr 25, 2025\n\nIt looks like wildcard redirect uris for applications are not working properly and result in an “Invalid redirect\\_uri” error. The hint for “ http://localhost/\\*” on the application management page suggests that wildcard redirect uris are supported.\n\nIn my case, “ https://app.sweatstack.no/\\*” didn’t work, but providing the full redirect uri did.\n\nAlthough some (most?) cases can be handled with the `state` query parameter, it would be nice to support wildcard redirect uris.\n\n## post by david on Apr 28, 2025\n\nI see. Yes wildcards are not supported. I will see what I can do about that.\n\n14 days later\n\n\n## post by Lady\\_Jesikat on May 13, 2025\n\nAlso, for localhost URIs, can the port number be ignored?"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Intervals’ own OAuth documentation confirms the last two causes: `WRITE` already includes `READ`, so requesting both for a scope is invalid, and its access tokens do not use refresh tokens or a one-hour lifetime. I’ll make the browser obtain the authorize URL through the authenticated API, request only non-conflicting scopes, and preserve tokens with no advertised expiry.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_rh9TULvUl5DXv8yg2RtIujpY","function":{"name":"TodoList","arguments":"{\"todos\":[{\"title\":\"Inspect OAuth code and connection logs\",\"status\":\"done\"},{\"title\":\"Fix connect and token refresh failures\",\"status\":\"in_progress\"},{\"title\":\"Verify the integration locally\",\"status\":\"pending\"},{\"title\":\"Document root causes in ANSWER.md\",\"status\":\"pending\"}]}"}}]}
